  <h2>The human-AI sandwich (why you should never just copy-paste AI output)</h2>
  <p>The idea Anita kept coming back to is what she calls the human-AI sandwich. You start with the human element, you tell AI what you want, you give it context, you give it your messy thinking. Then you let AI do a fair chunk of the work. Then you bring it back and humanise it again before it ever goes out the door.</p>
  <blockquote>"AI should never think for us, it should think with us."</blockquote>
  <p>That last step matters more than people think. You can absolutely train an AI to sound like you, but Anita still goes back through and changes the odd word, adds her own nuance, puts herself back into it. Never copy and paste AI verbatim. It can draft your copy. It shouldn't be the last voice in the room.</p>

  <h2>Claude.ai vs Claude Code vs the Chrome extension, what's actually different</h2>
  <p>There are three ways into Claude, and most people only ever meet the first one.</p>
  <p><strong>Claude.ai</strong> is the regular website or app version, and for most people, that's genuinely enough. <strong>Claude Code</strong> is a different interface entirely, it talks to the files on your computer in real time, which means it can actually build things: sales pages, waitlist pages, calculators, whole apps. You have to give it permission and set the parameters, but once it's trained, you can hand it real work.</p>
  <p>Then there's the <strong>Chrome extension</strong>, or in Anita's case, the Brave browser version. It sits in your sidebar and reads whatever site you're currently on. She used to open client websites in it and ask it to flag every broken link, every button, every piece of copy that needed work, an instant, unpaid research assistant. It's also just a lighter, faster way in: fewer steps between you and the answer means fewer tokens burned getting there.</p>

  <h2>Projects: the Claude feature most people skip</h2>
  <p>Inside Claude.ai, a Project is essentially its own island. Anything you upload or discuss inside it stays inside it, nothing leaks out, and nothing from your regular chats leaks in. Anita keeps her business branding in its own Project, her email marketing in another, so none of it gets muddied by whatever market research or random thought she's feeding Claude that day.</p>
  <p>The payoff is that you only have to explain who you are once. Upload a text file or markdown doc with your brand story, your offers, your voice, and every new chat inside that Project already knows it. You're not starting from zero every single time.</p>

  <h2>The small habits that stop your tokens running out</h2>
  <p>If you've ever wondered why you seem to run out of usage after "only" a few questions, it's because Claude reads the entire conversation from the start, every single time, including a chat that's been running for two weeks. A few things Anita does differently:</p>
  <p>She avoids feeding it PDFs, which are heavy on tokens, instead she copies the text out and drops that in directly. If she's working from a Google Doc, she downloads it as a markdown file first, which any AI reads far more efficiently. And when a chat gets long, she asks for a handoff summary and starts fresh rather than dragging the whole history along with her.</p>
  <h3>What this actually looks like in practice</h3>
  <p>Anita's membership sales page is the clearest example. She trained a copywriting skill on her own voice, gave it a brief, and let it draft the copy, that part took about 45 minutes to review and humanise. She fed the finished copy back into Claude Code, along with the images to choose from, and told it to build. Thirty-one minutes later, while she was having dinner, it was done. Padding, spacing, layout, the parts that usually eat entire days for someone who actually does web design, handled in under two hours, start to finish.</p>
  <p>She's also built an income calculator that reverse-engineers how many sales she needs to hit a goal, and a scheduled task that reads her end-of-day voice notes and emails her every Friday afternoon celebrating everything she got done that week. Small builds, real time back.</p>

